                     README for ps2 vector/matrix classes
		
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Introduction:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  This project contains C++ classes for 3d and 4d vector math using VU0, based 
  on the vector classes included with the ps2stuff project.  They use compiler 
  intrinsics instead of inline assembly, supported by GCC 3.2.  CodeWarrior 
  R3.5 Compiler 3.0.1 (in beta) supports the same syntax, but has not been 
  tested with this code.  An SN Systems version using intrinsics is also 
  planned -- changes may be necessary for their intrinsics syntax.
  
  Example:
    
    #include "ps2_vector.h"
    ...
    
    vec_xyz axyz, bxyz, cxyz;         // vec_xyz also typedef'ed to vec_3
    float f;
    
    axyz.set(1.0f, 2.0f, 3.0f);       // axyz = (1, 2, 3)
    bxyz.set(4.0f);                   // bxyz = (4, 4, 4)
    cxyz = axyz + bxyz;               // add vectors
    cxyz = axyz - bxyz;               // subtract vectors
    f = axyz.dot(bxyz);               // dot product
    cxyz = axyz.cross(bxyz);          // cross product
    cxyz = -axyz;                     // negate
    cxyz = axyz * 5.0f;               // scale by 5
    cxyz = axyz * bxyz.get_y();       // scale axyz by y element of bxyz
    cxyz = axyz.normalized();         // return normalized axyz
    axyz.normalize();                 // normalize axyz

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Vectors:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  
  The main classes are:
  
    vec_xyz (vec_3): 3d vector
    vec_xyzw (vec_4): 4d vector
    
  Arithmetic operators +, -, * are defined between vectors of the same type.
    
  Note: '*' means 'vmul', not dot or cross product (use .dot() or .cross())
  
  Operators +, -, *, / are also defined as broadcast operations with a scalar 
  right hand side:
  
    cxyz = axyz / bxyz.get_z();       // divide axyz by z element of bxyz

  When mixing types:
  
  You can only cast to (construct) a vector with fewer elements.
  
    vec_xyzw xyzw;
    vec_xyz  xyz;
    
    xyz = vec_xyz(xyzw);   // ok -- construct vec_xyz with x,y,z of vec_xyzw
    xyzw = vec_xyzw(xyz);  // error -- too few elements to set entire vector

  You can construct a vector from a scalar which broadcasts the value.
  
    xyz = vec_xyz( 3.0f );          // xyz = (3,3,3)
    xyz = vec_xyz( xyzw.get_w() );  // xyz = (xyzw.w,xyzw.w,xyzw.w)
    
  You can only assign to a vector with more elements.
  
    xyzw = xyz;           // ok -- set x,y,z fields of xyzw
    xyz = xyzw;           // error -- w field discarded
    xyz = vec_xyz(xyzw);  // ok -- explicit about loss of w

  Note: the set() methods match the constructors, not assignment.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Matrices:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The main matrix classes are:
  
    mat_33: 3x3 matrix
    mat_34: 3x4 matrix
    mat_43: 4x3 matrix
    mat_44: 4x4 matrix

  Matrix vector multiplies are matrix * vector, thinking of vectors as columns,
  and coordinate axes as columns of a matrix.
  
  Example:
  
    #include "ps2_matrix.h"
    
    mat_33 M, N;
    vec_3  a, b, c;
    
    M.col0 = a; M.col1 = b; M.col2 = c;    
    N.set( a, b, c );
    
    c = M * a + N * b;
    
    M = N.transposed();   // M is assigned a transposed N
    N.transpose();        // transpose N
    M = N.inverse();      // M is assigned the inverse of N
    N.invert();           // invert N

  Note:  We know some people think of vectors as rows, coordinate axes as 
  rows of a matrix, and operations as vector * matrix.  The data and method 
  implementations are identical in both cases, it really is just the operand 
  order that's different.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Points, Vectors, and Transforms:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  point_t and vector_t types distinguish 3d points and vectors.

    point_t  pt;
    vector_t vec;
    
    pt = pt + vec;  // ok -- can add a point and vector
    pt = pt + pt;   // error -- can't add two points
    vec = pt - pt;  // ok -- difference of two points is a vector

  Casts between vector_t, point_t and vec_3 shouldn't cost anything - the casts 
  make a 128-bit copy that the compiler can optimize away.  

  transform_t contains a 3x3 matrix and a translation vector.  Transforming by 
  a point_t includes the translation, multiplying by a vector_t omits it.
        
    vec_xyz xyz;
    transform_t xfrm;
      
    pt = xfrm * pt;     // apply 3x3 matrix and translation
    vec = xfrm * vec;   // apply 3x3 matrix
  
  A multiplication operator with vec_xyz isn't defined, since it's not clear 
  whether to use the translation.  You could use casts to be specific:
  
    xyz = vec_xyz( xfrm * vector_t(xyz) );  // apply 3x3 matrix
    xyz = vec_xyz( xfrm * point_t(xyz) );   // apply 3x3 matrix and translation
    
  However these methods are easier, and let you avoid the vector_t and point_t 
  types completely:
  
    xyz = xfrm.mul_vector(xyz);  // apply 3x3 matrix
    xyz = xfrm.mul_point(xyz);   // apply 3x3 matrix and translation
     
  A transform_t can be inverted assuming an arbitrary 3x3 matrix, or with a 
  faster routine that assumes the 3x3 matrix is orthogonal (a rotation)
    
    xfrm.invert();              // general 3x3
    xfrm.orthogonal_invert();   // orthogonal 3x3 - faster

  These types can be thought of in terms of homogeneous coordinates: a point_t 
  is a 4d vector with a last element of 1, a vector_t is a 4d vector with a 
  last element of 0.  However, the last element is never accessed and is a 
  random value - methods are all written to assume the 0 or 1 value.  (Casts 
  from a point_t or vector_t to a vec_4 will set the vec_4's w to a 0 or 1).  
  Likewise, a transform_t is a 4x4 matrix with a implicit last row of 
  (0, 0, 0, 1). (A transform_t is technically an 'affine' transform.)  
  One benefit over general 4x4 matrices is that they can be composed faster.
    
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Single-element types (vec_x, vec_y, vec_z, and vec_w):
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The plan is that users can mostly ignore these - they're part of the 
  implementation, but the API doesn't require you refer to these in your code.
  
  Like other vectors, they are 128-bit values, but only one field is used.  
  Math operations with them broadcast that field, using a broadcast 
  macroinstruction.
    
    vec_y   y;
    vec_xyz xyz;
    
    xyz = xyz * y;    // vec_xyz::operator * (vec_y) uses a 'vmuly'
  
  They're mostly intended for accessing a particular element of a 3d or 4d 
  vector:

    cxyz = axyz * vec_y(bxyz);    // scale axyz by y element of bxyz

  The cast doesn't really do anything other than select the desired * method.
  (The cast is defined as a copy which the compiler can avoid.)
  
  The get_?() methods basically do the same thing and return a single-element
  vector, but the type doesn't have to be mentioned.
   
    cxyz = axyz * bxyz.get_y();   // scale axyz by y element of bxyz
    
  The result can be treated as a float in most cases, since implicit conversions 
  between single-element types and floats are defined:
  
    float f = bxyz.get_y();       // get y element of bxyz as float

  It's possible that in ambiguous cases the compiler might complain and require 
  a cast.
  
  Also, a set_?() method can take a float, or any single-element type.
    
  Note: You can assign a single-element vector to a larger vector (as long as 
  it has that field), which sets only that field.  On the other hand, if you 
  construct or set() a vector with a single-element vector, that value is 
  broadcast to the entire vector.

    xyz = y;              // xyz.y = y
    xyz = vec_xyz(y);     // xyz = (y,y,y)

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Core Vectors:
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  
  core vector classes use only the EE core.  These are fairly basic at this 
  point, but they at least help with accessing vectors as floats, since there 
  are conversions between them:

    #include "core_vector.h"

    // get vec_3 elements.
    		
    vec_3      v;
    core_vec_3 cv;
    
    cv = core_vec_3(v);
    
    float x = cv.x;
    float y = cv.y;
    float z = cv.z;
  
  Indexing operators are defined, [] for a reference to a field, () for a copy.
  You should use them with compile-time constants only, since they use an if 
  statement to choose the right field (optimized away when the index is 
  constant).
  
  Note: Matrices are indexed as column/row

  #include "core_matrix.h"
  
  core_mat_44 M;
  M[1][2] = 5.0f; // set column 1, row 2
  
